  data:  # c1f5
    kind: work_item  # c1f5
    title: "metrics.total_files semantics ambiguous: 3 different 'file count' values on identical input (863 vs 1119 vs 809)"  # c1f5
    status: todo_hard  # c1f5
    priority: 2  # c1f5
    parent:  # c1f5
    tags:  # c1f5
    - profile  # c1f5
    - metrics  # c1f5
    - semantic_ambiguity  # c1f5
    - self_analysis  # c1f5
    - dogfooding  # c1f5
    isbefore: []  # c1f5
    duplicate_of: []  # c1f5
    not_duplicate_of: []  # c1f5
    pr_ref:  # c1f5
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 8).**\n\n**Symptom.** Three different \"file count\" numbers in the same behavior-map JSON, all answering \"how many files?\" differently and inconsistently:\n\n| Definition | Value (self-analysis) | Producer |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `metrics.total_files` | 863 | `metrics.py:42-43` — `len({n['path'] for n in nodes if n['path']})` |\n| Sum of `profile.languages[*].files` (after de-duping shell alias) | 1,119 | `profile.py:_detect_languages` — extension-based scan |\n| Count of `node.kind == \"file\"` Symbols | 809 | Analyzer-emitted file-kind Symbols |\n\nNone of the three agree. There is no documentation in the schema (`docs/schema.json`) or spec (`docs/hypergumbo-spec.md`) explaining what `total_files` means, how it differs from per-language sums, or which one a consumer should use for \"files in this repo.\"\n\n**Why each number disagrees.**\n\n- **863 (`metrics.total_files`):** counts unique `path` fields across ALL Symbol nodes, including the `<external>` sentinel (counted as one \"file\") and including every path-containing Symbol — function-kind, class-kind, method-kind, plus the file-kind ones. Excludes files that contributed zero symbols (binary, unparseable, unsupported language).\n\n- **1,119 (per-language sum):** extension-based file census via `find_files`. Includes files the analyzer rejected (binary `.py`, malformed source). Double-counts files when extensions map to multiple languages (e.g., `.h` → C/C++/Objective-C, though the shell-alias double-count is the only one observed on self-analysis after de-duping that ~10 files).\n\n- **809 (file-kind Symbols):** counts Symbols emitted with `kind == \"file\"` specifically. Only analyzers that follow the file-symbol convention contribute. Self-analysis: 809 happens to equal exactly the Python file count, suggesting only py.py emits file-kind Symbols today (consistent with INV-sopon's evidence that linker file-Symbol emission is uneven — and INV-ronuf showing some linkers create phantom file-Symbols, which would push this number above 809 if they all participated correctly).\n\n**Severity.** Low-medium. The risk is consumer confusion. An agent reading `total_files: 863` reasonably infers \"863 files in this repo.\" That number doesn't match disk reality (>1,000 source files), doesn't match the per-language sum, and doesn't match the file-Symbol count. Cross-checks against `total_files` will mislead.\n\n**Fix proposal — two viable directions.**\n\n**Option A: pick one canonical, drop the other two.**\n\nDecide `total_files` means \"extension-matched files on disk after default-excludes\" (the per-language sum, after R8-1 alias dedup). Update `metrics.py` to compute it that way and document it. Don't expose the other two in metrics.\n\nPro: simplest consumer surface; one number for \"files in repo.\"\nCon: loses the \"analyzer reach\" signal that the current `total_files` definition captures.\n\n**Option B: expose all three with explicit names.**\n\nRename to disambiguate:\n- `metrics.unique_paths_in_analysis: 863`\n- `metrics.files_by_extension: 1119`\n- `metrics.analyzed_file_symbols: 809`\n\nDrop the ambiguous `total_files`. Add a section to `docs/hypergumbo-spec.md` explaining when to use each.\n\nPro: each number is honest about what it represents.\nCon: more JSON surface; verbose; risk of \"which one do I use?\" decision fatigue for agents.\n\nRecommended: **A for the agent-facing schema** (`total_files = files_by_extension`, post-alias-dedup), **B for a `metrics.debug` sub-block** for tooling that needs the introspection.\n\n**Test gap.** Add an assertion that on the hypergumbo self-analysis fixture, `metrics.total_files` matches a known ground-truth value computed via `find_files(...)` directly. Right now there's no end-to-end check that the file count means what consumers think it means.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/metrics.py:25-95` (current `total_files` definition)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/profile.py:737-768` (per-language sum source)\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/ir.py` (file-kind Symbol convention)\n- `INV-sopon-...` (linker Symbol stamping unevenness — relates to file-Symbol count behavior)\n- `INV-ronuf-...` (phantom file Symbols from websocket linker — relates to file-Symbol count being inflated for some inputs)\n- `INV-kalif-...` (sketch `<external>` leak — sibling case of synthetic-path leakage into a consumer-facing count)"  # c1f5
    fields: {}  # c1f5
